WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.307 --> 00:00:19.848
[MUSIC - "THE BALLAD OF JOHN
 HENRY"]

00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:24.785
John Henry when we was
a baby, sitting down

00:00:24.785 --> 00:00:31.631
on his mami's knee, picked up a
hammer in his little right hand

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:35.054
and that little
piece of steel said,

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:39.960
the hammer will be
the death of me.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.160
Dr. [? Wiesner ?]
received his doctorate

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.490
in electrical engineering from
the University of Michigan.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.060
It was Archibald
Macleish, then head

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.030
of the Library of
Congress, who invited

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.880
him to establish and operate
a recording and acoustics

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.640
laboratory within the library.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.380
And now, 30 years
later, what would

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.440
Archibald Macleish
say of Jerome Wiesner?

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.470
Ladies and gentlemen,
I asked Mr. Macleish

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:11.170
to step to the lectern.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:15.680
I have called these lines
speech for an occasion,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.150
the occasion
obviously is this one.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.620
And I have dedicated them to the
occasion of the occasion, Jerry

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.820
[? Wiesner. ?]
Rinsing our mouths

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.680
with praise, tin cup
but a line of stones

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.720
spring in the cool
of a mint bed.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.380
Earlier generations knew this
place, made their way here,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.002
thronging.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.260
And we have forgotten it.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.820
We have kept to the
streets too long,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.960
tongues stale, hearts thirsty.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.540
Oh, to praise.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.150
God's will in the world,
if we could learn it,

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:02.350
test it on our lips
with taste of praise.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.440
Why else should the
world be beautiful?

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.100
Why should the leaves look as
they do, the light, the water?

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.500
Rinsing our mouths with
praise of a good man.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.580
I say what I mean.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.760
I do not say a good
man in a bad time.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.170
All the times are bad
when the man fails them.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.720
I say a good man at a
time when men are scarce,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.470
when the intelligent foregather,
follow each other around

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.980
in a fog like sheep
bleat in the rain,

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.040
complain because
Godo never comes,

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.870
because all life is
a tragic absurdity,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.310
Sisyphus sweating
away at his stone,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:53.270
and this rock won't because
freedom and dignity.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.520
Oh, weep, they say, for
freedom and dignity.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.500
You're not free.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.610
It's your grandfather's
ich you're scratching.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:01.790
You have no dignity.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:02.920
You're not a man.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.040
You're wrapped in a vat of
rewards and punishments.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.200
You think you've
chosen the rewards.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.310
You haven't.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.560
The rewards have chosen you.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.460
I weep.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.660
Rinsing our mouths with
praise of a good man in a time

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.990
when men are scarce
when the word chirps

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.870
like a cricket on the cellar
floor, on the damp stone,

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:31.770
and the mind maunders.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:35.230
A good man, look him
there in the fog.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.340
Look, he saunters along to his
place in the world's weather,

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.220
lights his pipe,
hitches his pants,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.400
talks back to accepted opinions.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.800
Congressional
committees hear him say,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.270
and not what you think,
what you haven't thought of.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.220
He addresses presidents.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.210
He says governments even
now still have to govern.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:02.260
No one is going to invent
a self-governing Holocaust.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.110
The Pentagon receives his views.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.490
Science, he says, is no
substitute for thought.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:14.140
Miracle drugs perhaps
not, miracle wars.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:17.500
Adviser to presidents,
the papers call him.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.690
Advisor, I say, to the young.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.420
It's the young who
need competent friends,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:28.170
bold companions, honest
men who want to run out,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.660
won't write off mankind, sell up
the country, quick the venture,

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.690
jive the ship.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.340
You'll see if NBC comes in
in Cambridge off that drive.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.505
It fell under Cambridge police.

00:04:41.505 --> 00:04:43.896
Let's just help each other.

00:04:43.896 --> 00:04:44.860
Greetings.

00:04:47.752 --> 00:04:50.590
Did professor from Berkeley
come to give us help?

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.400
Where' Ben?

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.740
Ben is up at the island.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.030
Oh, up where the accident was.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.310
One in a car.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.040
Yeah.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.380
And he will come and
say what the rights are

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.450
and tell them all of this--

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.170
one car, one cop.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.795
And that will be sometime within
the next five to 10 minutes.

00:05:07.795 --> 00:05:10.680
[INAUDIBLE] you'd better
stay the fuck out of the way

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.648
because people are going
to be coming out way, OK?

00:05:13.648 --> 00:05:16.624
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:05:23.072 --> 00:05:25.552
[CHANTING]

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.810
You don't have to
actually go out and ask

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.090
every student, what's your
opinion on this issue.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:44.506
A lot of them
aren't going to have

00:05:44.506 --> 00:05:46.310
opinions on any random
issue that you pick.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:47.560
But you've got to
at least say, we

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.685
are about to make a decision
on this kind of thing.

00:05:49.685 --> 00:05:52.450
Anybody who's interested,
please submit comments.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.059
Now three years
ago, this institute

00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:55.600
went through a big
decision of should

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.670
we have a medical school.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:58.810
I didn't know about it.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:02.230
I've been here five years, and
all the time I was here nobody

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:03.880
even suggested
that we were going

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.920
through that kind
of a discussion.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.240
Why not?

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.290
Why won't student--

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:09.070
Why, oh, why not?

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:09.990
Yeah.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.130
If we had to answer
for [? that, ?]

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.810
and because we had--

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.522
it was obvious that
we couldn't afford to.

00:06:17.522 --> 00:06:18.980
I made a big hell
of a lot of sense

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.572
to have a big exercise
in frustration.

00:06:21.572 --> 00:06:23.530
Should we or shouldn't
we have a medical school

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.321
that we didn't have
$100 million that we're

00:06:25.321 --> 00:06:26.530
going to take the bill.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:28.150
The only way we could
say yes is if we

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.670
could find somebody who
would match the government

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:35.710
money with the promise
of $50 million cash.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:39.730
And Dr. Killian and I
just said, let's try.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.440
And then we-- and we hit
five people that we knew,

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.519
or six people.

00:06:44.519 --> 00:06:45.060
I don't know.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.851
And then we finally
talked to [? people, ?]

00:06:46.851 --> 00:06:49.120
some we couldn't
reach, who we thought

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.160
were interested enough
in medical schools, MIT,

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.430
and the whole thing
to see whether anyone

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.547
was willing to say, sure.

00:06:56.547 --> 00:07:01.360
[LAUGHS] And nobody did it.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.280
This was five days
after Howard became

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:05.560
president of this institution.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.914
Six months before, we had
been totaling up the bill

00:07:08.914 --> 00:07:11.080
on what we thought it was
going-- what we were going

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.900
to have to raise to do the
things we thought already had

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.520
to be done to keep
the promises that

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.340
had been made, like dormitories
and new buildings this time.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.520
And that came to $200
million that we didn't have.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.150
Know And I don't see why
that kind of a decision

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.229
has to be put to a
popular referendum.

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:30.520
I don't think it has to be
put to a popular referendum.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.820
I think you just have to
say that you're making it--

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.280
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:36.760
Who knows?

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.343
One of the students might have
known somebody with $50 million

00:07:39.343 --> 00:07:40.950
in his back pocket.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.350
I mean-- I mean, that's all.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.160
We're faced with a question
of whether the decisions about

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.770
today make sense for
this kind of group.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.390
I think it's fine to go
into the other things.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.290
But I think we also want to
come to some understanding as to

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.870
whether or not we're
involved in a decision making

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.790
process that makes sense, or
is this another medical school

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.690
deal?

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.890
I love this man.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:10.260
I rinse my mouth with his
praise in a frightened time.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.994
The taste in the cup is
of mint, of spring water.

00:08:15.994 --> 00:08:18.982
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.810
Thank you very
much, Mr. Macleish.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.730
And now, as chairmen
of the corporation

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.544
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology,

00:08:58.544 --> 00:09:01.530
and on behalf of
that body, I shall

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.720
proceed to induct into
office our 13 presidents.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.580
Will Dr. Killian and
Dr. [? Stretten ?]

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.760
please bring him forward.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:22.240
By the authority
of the corporation

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.396
and with the enthusiastic
approval of the faculty,

00:09:26.396 --> 00:09:28.883
the alumni, and
the student body,

00:09:28.883 --> 00:09:32.290
and of this
distinguished assembly,

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:37.820
I present into your keeping this
charter of the Massachusetts

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.790
Institute of Technology
and invest you

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:43.970
with all of the
authority, privileges,

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:47.990
and responsibilities of
the office of President.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:50.250
May you, Jerry,
serve the Institute

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.510
and dignify its
office of President

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:57.420
with all of the skill
and wisdom and dedication

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.400
which our confidence
accords to you.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:05.632
And may your administration
be memorable.

00:10:05.632 --> 00:10:08.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:39.860
Thank you, MIT's bright
presidents, an old friend,

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.357
[? Archibald ?] [? McKleish, ?]
for this honor and this

00:10:43.357 --> 00:10:44.211
challenge.

00:10:44.211 --> 00:10:47.520
And you, [? Paul ?] [? Gray, ?]
as you become a legal

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.040
chancellor, for being willing
to share the burden and joys

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:55.270
of leading this
great institution.

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:59.270
And a special thank to
Priscilla Gray and to my wife,

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:04.470
[? Leah, ?] for so willingly
taking the plunge with us

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:10.740
into the unknown and exciting
but obviously demanding future.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:16.145
Dear friends, thank you
all for this occasion.

00:11:16.145 --> 00:11:20.063
And I might just say
in passing, Dr. Killian

00:11:20.063 --> 00:11:24.120
has made me realize that I
had something to look for to--

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.820
retirement.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

